A split projective arrangement is a finite set of codimension-one linear subspaces of a vector space of dimension $`n+1`, regarded as hyperplanes in its projectivization.
For $`H_0\in\mathcal{P}` with equation $`\ell_0`, the slice $`\ell_0=1` is an affine $`n`-space, and intersecting the other hyperplanes with it gives the decone arrangement.

The slice depends on $`\ell_0` and not only on $`H_0`, so in the formalization the decone is indexed by $`\ell_0`.
The blueprint leaves the ring of $`U_{\mathcal{P}}` implicit, and the declarations of this box include it.
:::

:::node "lem:decone" (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.deconeEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.deconeEquiv_ratio_mul) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv_algebraMap_coord) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv_toCone) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv_symm_T) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.isHyperplane_comap_chart_slice) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.comap_chart_slice_inj) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.complementOpen_le_basicOpen) (decl := OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.deconeIso) (decl := OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.coneIso) (decl := OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.mapUnits_coneIso_hom_complementUnit)
The projective complement is the decone complement, and there is an isomorphism $`V_{\mathcal{P}}\simeq U_{\mathcal{P}}\times\mathbb{G}_m` given by $`v\mapsto(v/\ell_0(v),\ell_0(v))` with inverse $`(u,t)\mapsto tu`.
Under it, $`\ell_i=t(\ell_i/\ell_0)`.
These are identities of regular maps, not merely of rational points.
Distinct hyperplanes other than $`H_0` restrict to distinct nonempty affine hyperplanes in the slice.

In the formalization these are isomorphisms of coordinate rings, and $`\mathbb{G}_m` is the Laurent polynomial ring.
:::

:::node "lem:central-boundary" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_relations) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_generator) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_boundary) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_word) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_mul_of_mem_bigrading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.isHomogeneousOfShift_boundary)
For a central arrangement, the word boundary descends to a $`B`-linear map $`\partial` on the Orlik–Solomon algebra of bidegree $`(-1,-1)`, with $`\partial e_i=1`, $`\partial^2=0` and $`\partial(xy)=\partial(x)y+(-1)^px\partial(y)` for $`x` of cohomological degree $`p`.

Centrality here means that the hyperplanes have a common point, so it is independent of the origin used to identify an affine space with a vector space.
The boundary is a graded antiderivation with signs and is defined over every commutative coefficient ring, including characteristic two and the zero ring.
:::

:::node "lem:projective-algebra" (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.cone_isCentral) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveAlgebra) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.mem_projectiveAlgebra_iff) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveAlgebra_eq_adjoin) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveBigrading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveAlgebra_isHomogeneous) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveBigradingGradedAlgebra) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveDecomposition) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveDecomposition_apply) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.coe_projectiveProjection) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.coe_projectiveBoundary) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveDecompositionComponentEquiv)
For $`A=\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})`, define $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})=\ker\partial`.
It is a graded $`B`-subalgebra, generated by the differences $`e_i-e_0` for any chosen label $`0`, and $`(b,c)\mapsto b+e_0c` is a module equivalence from two copies of it to $`A`, with the second summand shifted by $`(1,1)`.

The algebraic statements apply to every central arrangement, without requiring projective geometry or a cohomology theory.
The inverse of the module equivalence is $`a\mapsto(a-e_0\partial a,\partial a)`, and the component equivalence records the shift on the inherited $`R`-submodule pieces of the weighted bigrading.
:::

:::node "lem:gm-product" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.gmProductEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.gmProductEquiv_apply) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.gmProductEquiv_mem_grading_iff) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.map_laurentSection_gmProductEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.stdResidue_gmProductEquiv)
For suitable $`X`, with projection $`p:X\times\mathbb{G}_m\to X` and second coordinate $`t`, the map $`(b,c)\mapsto p^*b+[t]\,p^*c` is a $`B`-linear equivalence from $`C(X)\oplus C(X)` to $`C(X\times\mathbb{G}_m)`, with the second summand shifted by $`(1,1)`.
Pullback along the section $`t=1` recovers $`b`, and the residue recovers $`c`.

In the formalization, every smooth $`X` is suitable.
:::

:::node "thm:projective" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.map_projectiveToCone_projectiveEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveRealization_unique) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveEquiv_eq_map_section) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveEquiv_difference) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.logClass_projectiveRatioUnit_eq_of_ker_eq) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveEquiv_symm_logClass_ratio) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.isHomogeneousOfShift_projectiveEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.isHomogeneousOfShift_projectiveEquiv_symm) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveComponentEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveDiagonalEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveDiagonalEquiv_difference) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveDiagonalEquiv_of_mem_degree)
For a nonempty split projective arrangement there is a canonical bigrading-preserving equivalence from $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})` to $`C(U_{\mathcal{P}})`, sending $`e_i-e_j` to $`[\ell_i/\ell_j]`.
It is independent of the equations and of the hyperplane chosen at infinity.
On diagonal components it gives the canonical graded $`R`-algebra equivalence from the projective algebra over $`R` to $`\bigoplus_{q\geq0} H^{q,q}(U_{\mathcal{P}})`.

The map is uniquely characterized by compatibility with affine realization after pullback to the cone.
The section $`t=1` makes this pullback injective, and differences descend because their logarithms are ratios.
For surjectivity, pull back to any decone and use the already proved logarithmic generation there.
Thus the hyperplane at infinity occurs in the proof, while the resulting map is canonical.
The diagonal theorem uses coefficient extension from $`R` and the affine diagonal theorem on the cone; injectivity detects the boundary kernel without a flatness hypothesis.
:::

:::node "cor:projective-betti" (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.poincarePolynomial) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.poincarePolynomial_eq_decone) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.poincarePolynomial_cone) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.bettiNumber_one) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveDeconeComponentEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.exists_logMonomialBasis_decone) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.nonempty_projective_grading_linearEquiv_bettiNumber) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.nonempty_basis_projective_grading_bettiNumber) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.nonempty_basis_projective_grading_one)
The projective Betti polynomial is the decone polynomial and satisfies $`P_{c\mathcal{P}}(t)=(1+t)P_{\mathcal{P}}(t)`.
In particular $`b_1(U_{\mathcal{P}})=|\mathcal{P}|-1`.

The polynomial identity is proved directly from the subset sum by pairing subsets with and without the hyperplane at infinity.
Pullback to any decone then identifies the actual cohomological pieces with those of that affine arrangement.
Thus for $`p\geq0`, $`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{P}})` is a direct sum of $`b_p` copies of $`B_{w-p}`, and $`H^{q,q}` has an $`R`-basis with exactly $`b_q` vectors.
These module equivalences and bases require choices.
Over the zero coefficient ring they hold for every cardinality, so they do not determine $`b_q`.
Here $`b_q` is the polynomial coefficient, which counts the words of length $`q` in a recursive logarithmic basis of any decone.
The word-count theorem records this count, though over the zero ring its existence statement alone also holds for other counts.
:::
